#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "287a493f-e988-5cef-b0d0-4205d1eaf74e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6313" :question/problem "Find a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} (y^2-x) \\, \\textrm ds,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = \\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\le \\theta \\le\\dfrac{ \\pi}4.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta \\sin{\\theta}$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}4.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) =y^2-x$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{ }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta (-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"}] :question/id #uuid "63aa09fb-9839-5f73-9f98-2eb9784ead43" :question/math-academy-id "e-6314" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac\\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $y=\\sin\\theta$, where $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}2.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-(0-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151951 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "00b29cf2-6a73-5fd6-b8bd-04ba8c5f1a46" :question/math-academy-id "e-6315" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} xy(1-x^2) \\,\\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the polar curve $r=\\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) = xy(1-x^2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\cos\\theta,$ $\\textrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\textrm d \\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}-{u}^{3}(1-{u}^{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{3}-{u}^{7})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{u}^{8}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{8})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90da3ca8-37af-554f-8d91-e57306848905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150942} {:answer/id #uuid "de4056d5-7ff6-55aa-af85-aaa30b96ed06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150943} {:answer/id #uuid "ee13be1c-1128-5cce-998b-e98cd7d59b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150944} {:answer/id #uuid "f52ea6ad-1753-56f5-98f8-95a2393d4afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150945} {:answer/id #uuid "78cdb22c-fa67-56b1-8f35-50e08de970e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150946}] :answer-field/id #uuid "61d6f4e9-3b02-5c82-a9e2-09f0904ced68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150941}] :question/id #uuid "64562ab0-7f9a-5e97-a09e-6e014afb5d25" :question/math-academy-id "q-76339" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)xyds$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r = 5\\sin θ$ for $0 \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c898e58d-93bd-5b7b-83af-ffbae5bf00d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\sqrt{2} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150960} {:answer/id #uuid "e263b7fb-2215-5b98-9d2b-d1c9a806484a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150961} {:answer/id #uuid "1958b138-6b04-5333-b9d2-5760b56a84ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150962} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8f81c6-87c9-58bf-8223-925f3a6025dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150963} {:answer/id #uuid "40179b66-de19-50e7-a649-f18747b267a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150964}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bccab8a0-46e0-5cbd-b014-7b53de12212d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150959}] :question/id #uuid "e2100053-2a05-53a9-93f0-e3b2f1998524" :question/math-academy-id "q-76353" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = x + y$ along the polar curve $r = 2$ for $-\\frac{π}{4} \\le θ \\le 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7eee1f5-4e67-5791-ab19-b18c7b3e839f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343993} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc4b0b3-3161-5573-8ff6-5f7ebe4c7119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344166} {:answer/id #uuid "e210fd40-0385-5c02-968e-6174892fbc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344228} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c07b9f-3789-507f-97de-8e306b0fb0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344384} {:answer/id #uuid "f5abf403-18d1-5fd6-8efd-732e33e8d35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3c07b9f-3789-507f-97de-8e306b0fb0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344384} :answer-field/id #uuid "7df0e8e5-c868-5ece-818b-a9164009ba5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d3824d0-05f9-53c5-8fe3-dac14910620a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76694" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{3}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}4\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5/2}-{0}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{5}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
